00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:33.420
A useful principle in the story of life is
that you should never underestimate algae

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.269
or cyanobacteria.

00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:41.520
They will always manage to surprise you,
and more importantly, to remind you that everything

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.935
you have, ultimately comes down to them.

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:47.231
Do you like oxygen?

00:00:47.231 --> 00:00:50.629
Well, take some time to thank a cyanobacterium.

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:55.200
If their ancestors hadn’t gone a little
haywire with their photosynthesis and set

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.452
off the Great Oxidation Event billions of
years ago, we would not exist.

00:01:01.452 --> 00:01:04.795
But you don’t even have to look back into
history to see the influence of

00:01:04.795 --> 00:01:06.731
algae and cyanobacteria.

00:01:06.731 --> 00:01:12.239
Today, even algae of the smallest size have
a tremendous impact on our lives, 

00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:14.310
both good and bad.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.060
On one hand, they translate sunlight into
food through photosynthesis.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.890
On the other hand, they bloom in waters and
release toxins that can wreak damage on the

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.055
organisms around them.

00:01:26.055 --> 00:01:31.190
But the real reason you should never underestimate
algae or cyanobacteria is because at their

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:37.390
core, they are organisms that seem to just
always find a way.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:43.081
They exist in super salty waters, in ponds,
in hot springs.

00:01:43.081 --> 00:01:49.360
Though, these are of course aquatic organisms, and 
so you might think of their adaptations as restricted

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.920
to watery environments.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.960
But what has made algae so capable of existing
beyond those boundaries is their willingness

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.000
to use other organisms as a means to their
own survival.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.450
At times, their symbiotic relationships sound
absurd, like the species that makes a home

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.569
out of sloth fur.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:15.709
But our favorite example of the creativity
that algae and cyanobacteria approach their

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:18.770
relationships with has to be lichen—

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:24.900
that combination of fungus and algae, or fungus
and cyanobacteria, that attaches to trees

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.989
and rocks and anything else that sits around
long enough.

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:31.940
The concept of a lichen appears simple enough.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.430
There’s a fungus, which has an incredible
capacity for spreading and decomposing, but

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.985
no means to make its own food.

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:45.349
And there’s the algae or cyanobacteria,
fully capable of making their own food, but

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.459
in need of some help getting around on land.

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:56.540
The result is called a dual organism, made
up of the fungal mycobiont and the algal photobiont

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.663
that takes on the spreading qualities of the
first, and the colors of the second.

00:03:04.476 --> 00:03:09.850
Fungi and algae can meet and work together
in ways that actually don’t result in a lichen.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:14.860
The distinct structure that we associate with
lichens comes down to the thallus, made up

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:20.550
of distinct layers that reveal the different
combinations of the organisms that make it.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:26.549
The thallus is what allows lichens to survive,
keeping the organism alive in dry conditions

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:30.160
so it can tolerate extremes in humidity and
temperature.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.739
The shape is generally a function of the fungus,
which starts to weave its body into the appropriate

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:40.189
layers as soon as it comes upon algae to team
up with.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:46.580
At the edge is the cortex, where fungal cells
are thicker and close in contact so they can

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.030
form a protective structure.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:54.850
And beneath, it keeps a more fibrous and loose
mass of hyphae, a scaffolding structure called

00:03:54.850 --> 00:04:01.739
the medulla that the photobionts layer themselves
on, dividing and creating their own algal

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:07.259
layer that’s large and green and positioned
to access the sun.

00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:13.150
That layer of cells tells us a little bit more about
the organisms inside the lichen, though only

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.949
under the right conditions.

00:04:14.949 --> 00:04:16.739
A dry lichen is typically gray.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:22.880
But with water, the fungal gray clears up,
revealing the color of the photobionts inside

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.000
the lichen.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.152
In the case of algae photobionts, you’ll
typically see a bright green color.

00:04:28.152 --> 00:04:33.540
But when there are cyanobacteria, the hues
vary from dark green to black.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.459
Now lichens have to actually stick to the
thing they are stuck to,

00:04:37.459 --> 00:04:41.233
and they have a few ways to anchor
themselves onto surfaces.

00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:46.690
Some have a structure called a holdfast that
extends as part of the thallus, a bit like

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.720
a peg that is very good at holding on to things
like rock.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.244
Our lichen here though uses a different type of 
structure called rhizines.

00:04:55.244 --> 00:05:01.360
These are threads that come from the medulla
or lower cortex, creeping out from the lichen

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.144
like roots, only they do not have any 
capacity to draw in nutrients.

00:05:06.144 --> 00:05:08.393
They are just anchors.

00:05:08.393 --> 00:05:13.077
Now from our perspective right now, there’s 
nothing controversial about this partnering of

00:05:13.077 --> 00:05:14.173
fungus and algae.

00:05:14.173 --> 00:05:19.319
We look in the lichen, we see signs of both
organisms, and we boil it down to a simple

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:22.220
exchange of protection for food.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:28.460
This ancient relationship brought algae onto
land and into places so different from their

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.236
watery origins.

00:05:30.236 --> 00:05:37.100
But our understanding of lichens as not just
one organism, but two is actually relatively recent,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:43.509
going back only to 1867, when a Swiss botanist
named Simon Schwendener presented his work

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.303
at the Swiss Natural History Society.

00:05:46.303 --> 00:05:52.970
He’d been looking at lichen under the microscope,
just as we are now, when he considered the possibility

00:05:52.970 --> 00:06:00.668
that the mass of hyphae he was looking at 
belonged to a fungus, wrapped around algae.

00:06:00.668 --> 00:06:05.087
His contemporary lichenologists did not agree.

00:06:05.087 --> 00:06:10.289
Sure, Schwendener was just one of multiple
scientists who had noted a resemblance between

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:15.020
the parts of lichen, and the bodies of fungi
and algae.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.639
But that was just a resemblance, it seemed
to the lichenologists at the time.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:24.454
In their defense, they did not know that
a dual organism could exists,

00:06:24.454 --> 00:06:27.509
They did not know that there was a symbiosis here

00:06:27.509 --> 00:06:33.760
How could they, when the word symbiosis
hadn’t been invented yet?

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.592
Schwendener had more reasons than just appearances
though.

00:06:37.592 --> 00:06:43.020
One challenge that lichenologists repeatedly
came across was that it was impossible to

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:50.557
grow a new lichen from isolating either the
spores or the green cells growing within the lichen.

00:06:50.557 --> 00:06:58.060
But in 1877, a scientist named Albert Bernhard
Frank came up with the term “symbiosis.”

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.400
The organism that inspired him to come up
with that word?

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.465
Well of course., it was lichen.

00:07:04.465 --> 00:07:09.759
Frank was one of several biologists intrigued
by the possibility of Schwendener’s hypothesis,

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:15.876
this idea that organisms could live together,
not just in an environment, but in one entity.

00:07:16.754 --> 00:07:23.789
Now with time, lichenologists came around, and
people only uncovered more examples and nuances

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:25.532
around symbiosis.

00:07:25.532 --> 00:07:30.108
But that does not mean that we 
actually understand lichen.

00:07:30.108 --> 00:07:35.180
They remain challenging to cultivate in a
lab, and we’re still understanding whether

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:40.420
the symbiosis we’ve reduced to a simple
meeting of two organisms might  actually more

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:44.960
complex—whether it can involve other species
as well.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.639
Perhaps there’s a way to describe lichens
and the world inside of them that we just

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:56.840
don’t know yet, a word that, like symbiosis,
will reveal something new and remarkable to

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.871
us about our world.

00:07:58.871 --> 00:08:04.606
The way we talk about science and knowledge
 sometimes makes it feel like we’re done,

00:08:04.606 --> 00:08:08.729
that science is a static set of all of the facts
in the universe,

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:13.563
because it is a lot easier to talk about the things 
that we know than the things that we don’t.

00:08:13.563 --> 00:08:18.301
Of course it’s hard to talk about the things
that we don't know, because we don't know them.

00:08:18.301 --> 00:08:25.105
But our world is always there, containing
the information that we want in everyday

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:29.620
occurrences that are sometimes hundreds 
of millions of years old,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:36.103
we humans just have to figure out ways to 
ask the questions that will actually give us answers.

00:08:41.572 --> 00:08:47.219
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

